<edge source="iran_regional_conflict::proxy_attack_frequency" target="energy_market_stress::diesel_export_volume" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">A rising rate of proxy strikes against tankers, terminals or transport corridors forces shippers and refiners to suspend or reroute shipments and insurers to restrict coverage, directly lowering diesel export flows.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_regional_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">energy_market_stress</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_regional_conflict::proxy_attack_frequency" target="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_disruption" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained proxy strikes against commercial shipping and infrastructure in the Red Sea/Strait of Hormuz raise insurance costs and force rerouting, constraining alternative energy flows to Europe and thereby amplifying energy supply disruptions tied to the Russia–Ukraine conflict.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_regional_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_regional_conflict::proxy_attack_frequency" target="us_china_rivalry::export_control_strength" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher frequency of proxy strikes that investigators trace to components, dual‑use goods or financing routed through Chinese-linked companies prompts US policymakers to expand export controls and denial listings to block items vulnerable to diversion to proxies.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_regional_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_rivalry</data>
</edge>
