<edge source="iran_regional_conflict::sanctions_pressure" target="energy_market_stress::policy_export_restrictio" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalating sanctions on the energy and logistics sectors prompt affected governments to formalize or tighten diesel export controls to conserve domestic supply and limit sanction exposure.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_regional_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">energy_market_stress</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_regional_conflict::sanctions_pressure" target="global_bond_shock::long_term_nominal_yields" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Tightened sanctions that curb Iranian exports and/or constrain banking flows can push up global energy and risk premia and inflation expectations, raising long-term nominal sovereign yields via higher term premia and expected inflation.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_regional_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">global_bond_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_regional_conflict::sanctions_pressure" target="us_china_rivalry::capital_invest_flows" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Intensified sanctions on Iran, especially with aggressive secondary‑sanctions enforcement, raises compliance and counterparty risk for Chinese banks and investors, causing them to scale back outbound M&amp;A, portfolio and project financing that could expose them to sanctions, reducing cross‑border capital flows.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_regional_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_rivalry</data>
</edge>
