  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher on‑hand diesel inventories attenuate supply shocks and reduce the likelihood of abrupt price spikes, lowering cross‑market uncertainty and realized/implied volatility.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">energy_market_stress</data>
  <data key="d40">global_bond_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="energy_market_stress::diesel_inventory_levels" target="iran_regional_conflict::military_escalation" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.55</data>
  <data key="d37">Rapid depletion of diesel inventories constrains military and militia logistics and readiness, incentivizing preemptive operations, seizures of supply routes, or forceful actions to secure fuel, thereby raising the intensity of kinetic engagements.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">energy_market_stress</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_regional_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="energy_market_stress::diesel_inventory_levels" target="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_feedback" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher on‑hand diesel inventories at frontline depots reduce competition for scarce stocks and diminish incentives for pre‑emptive strikes over fuel, thereby weakening reciprocal attack–reprisal dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">energy_market_stress</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
